            <ArticleTitle>Percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty in dextrocardia: case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>140-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty (PTCA) is described in a patient with dextrocardia. Selective cannulation of the right anterior descending coronary artery (morphologic left anterior descending coronary artery) was achieved with modification of standard guiding catheters &quot;preformed&quot; for the normal, levo-position heart. This report demonstrates the feasibility of PTCA in patients with cardiac malposition who survive into adulthood and develop coronary atherosclerosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
